Right? Exactly. That's not a,

00:15:52.800 --> 00:15:55.160
that's the objective of the game.

00:15:56.040 --> 00:15:57.500
They're like, so here's the deal.

00:15:57.780 --> 00:15:59.160
All right. Like what's their story?

00:15:59.320 --> 00:16:00.280
They don't have a story.

00:16:00.420 --> 00:16:01.760
Okay. They just came to be.

00:16:02.200 --> 00:16:04.420
It's funny though, because of how much it,

00:16:04.440 --> 00:16:05.680
and I guess this is a point,

00:16:05.760 --> 00:16:07.920
like there's a lot of character going on.

00:16:07.920 --> 00:16:09.480
And even in just the way that,

00:16:10.160 --> 00:16:13.260
like after you eat any nibbly pibbly,

00:16:13.500 --> 00:16:16.140
he then, like there's like an actual,

00:16:16.480 --> 00:16:19.180
uh, there's,

00:16:19.240 --> 00:16:20.420
there's like a cool down,

00:16:20.700 --> 00:16:24.300
uh, because you have to spit out the legs.

00:16:24.300 --> 00:16:25.880
It's actually pretty hardcore.

00:16:56.820 --> 00:17:03.580
And in a way, this does, like, knowing what Rare will eventually do on Super Nintendo

00:17:03.580 --> 00:17:11.800
and N64, I do get those, like, platforming vibes that will come later in games like Banjo

00:17:11.800 --> 00:17:15.700
and Kazooie. I get them here, even though it is just, like, straight levels.

00:17:16.180 --> 00:17:18.000
For some reason, I feel it.

00:17:18.080 --> 00:17:19.560
And maybe it's because I know it's rare.

00:17:19.760 --> 00:17:21.320
But I feel it here.

00:17:21.440 --> 00:17:27.120
And specifically, since I mentioned Banjo-Kazooie, I just wanted to throw out that, like, I only

00:17:27.120 --> 00:17:31.120
know this in reverse, but I'm sure some people know it the other way, where they played Snake

00:17:31.120 --> 00:17:33.040
Rattle and Roll first and then Banjo-Kazooie.

00:17:33.040 --> 00:17:39.560
But it is funny that in Banjo-Kazooie, in the water levels, if you swim out too far,

00:17:40.840 --> 00:17:47.460
eventually a shark will just appear and his character speech bubble will also pop up first

00:17:47.460 --> 00:17:48.700
before you really see him.

00:17:48.820 --> 00:17:52.960
And he'll just be, like, saying that he's coming to get you and everything and this dramatic

00:17:52.960 --> 00:17:53.940
music will happen.

00:17:54.040 --> 00:17:58.860
And it scared me to the point where I would have to just drop the controller and run upstairs.

00:17:58.860 --> 00:18:04.340
I couldn't face the shark, but I couldn't swim back to shore.

00:18:05.120 --> 00:18:06.980
I just was paralyzed with fear.

00:18:07.240 --> 00:18:10.200
But that's what's happening in this game.

00:18:10.460 --> 00:18:12.180
So did you have some flashbacks?

00:18:12.500 --> 00:18:13.320
Like, yeah, I did.

00:18:13.420 --> 00:18:17.780
I did. But in this game, the second you land in the water, the shark's like, here I come.

00:18:17.780 --> 00:18:22.080
You know, the music starts ramping up, which is a blatant like...

00:18:22.080 --> 00:18:24.280
This game was you finally facing your fears.

00:18:24.580 --> 00:18:26.300
Yeah. I mean, because it was a lot easier.

00:18:26.600 --> 00:18:27.800
You knew he was always coming.

00:18:30.120 --> 00:18:32.280
And you saw him early on.

00:18:32.420 --> 00:18:34.660
Like, there was no, like, I'm going to get you a text bubble.

00:18:34.800 --> 00:18:36.900
Like, come on, that's pretty frightening stuff.

00:18:37.840 --> 00:18:38.820
It is frightening.

00:18:38.880 --> 00:18:41.880
And also, I appreciate that it's sort of a...

00:18:42.860 --> 00:18:48.820
It's a more forgiving situation than just, like, a pitfall and then falling to your death.

00:18:48.820 --> 00:18:52.440
And I would say that, like, oh, this game, like, you know, it lets you fail.

00:18:52.660 --> 00:18:58.920
But then this game also does have pitfalls that make you fall to your death in weird ways.

00:18:58.960 --> 00:19:00.060
Like, even when...

00:19:00.060 --> 00:19:06.720
Because we're talking about the verticality of the level, there will be situations where you think that you can jump down.

00:19:07.260 --> 00:19:09.000
And you actually can't.

00:19:09.400 --> 00:19:17.480
You will die from falling before you reach the bottom in, like, in lengths that you wouldn't really think.

00:19:17.480 --> 00:19:24.200
So you definitely have to sort of relearn what your range is as well.

00:19:25.140 --> 00:19:35.540
Yeah. And what I had gathered, I don't know how true this was, but, yeah, that there is a specific distance that will just cause fall death.

00:19:36.080 --> 00:19:47.320
And I did, like, test it enough times where I was like, okay, I do know now, like, not to try anything that doesn't look really obviously like I could land on it.

00:19:47.380 --> 00:19:49.420
Because I feel like any time I was like, can I land on that?

00:19:49.500 --> 00:19:51.160
I would jump and I would just fall by death.

00:19:51.720 --> 00:19:53.940
It's not, like, it's not egregious, though.

00:19:54.080 --> 00:19:55.540
Like, you eventually do learn that.

00:19:56.000 --> 00:20:02.940
Right. Another notable 3D isometric game on the NES is Marble Madness.

00:20:03.220 --> 00:20:08.940
And that was originally in the arcades but ported to the NES by Rare.

00:20:09.140 --> 00:20:13.560
So it's kind of interesting to think that they maybe had this system down.

00:20:13.560 --> 00:20:25.080
And Marble Madness is a game where, admittedly not a platformer, but you control a marble going through vertical isometric stages from top to bottom.

00:20:25.320 --> 00:20:30.780
And now they've made a game where you basically have to try to platform your way from bottom to top.

00:20:31.620 --> 00:20:37.560
Do you see any other similarities maybe between Marble Madness and Snake Rattle and Roll?

00:20:38.420 --> 00:20:44.760
Well, it's not exactly the same because Marble Madness is obviously, like, a lot more about this, like, momentum and speed.

00:20:44.940 --> 00:20:53.140
But it is one of the smoother feeling movements on an NES game that, like, I remember playing.

00:20:53.780 --> 00:20:59.120
And I think that that works, like, really well with what this game is trying to do.

00:20:59.320 --> 00:21:04.980
Like, I don't know, something about the movement and the graphical style, maybe because it's isometric and makes it easier to do this.

00:21:05.060 --> 00:21:06.140
But it's all just very clean.

00:21:06.140 --> 00:21:07.920
You know, it does feel...

00:21:07.920 --> 00:21:18.580
Now, despite the fact that there's the weird isometric controls and that makes it, like, a little tough, it otherwise feels ahead of its time in the controls.

00:21:18.760 --> 00:21:24.660
Like, if only the D-pad lined up with the directions you would go, then this would feel great.

00:21:24.800 --> 00:21:25.760
You know what I mean?

00:21:26.140 --> 00:21:29.940
I think that there was a little bit of just...

00:21:29.940 --> 00:21:37.080
I mean, obviously, they knew that it was going to feel weird just by the perspective.

00:21:37.180 --> 00:21:43.880
So they threw in what looks like a power-up, but is actually the thing you really don't want to pick up.

00:21:44.080 --> 00:21:53.800
Yeah. Which is, in addition to asymmetric controls, in addition to maybe not having a great grasp on height and depth,

00:21:54.520 --> 00:21:57.200
now your controls are reversed.

00:21:58.140 --> 00:22:00.420
And I remember seeing it for the first time.

00:22:00.520 --> 00:22:02.320
I was like, oh, that looks like a cool little power-up.

00:22:02.540 --> 00:22:07.780
And no, now if I press right, I'm going left, but also not really left.

00:22:07.960 --> 00:22:09.480
It's not really right.

00:22:09.580 --> 00:22:12.240
So I was like, oh, God, this is hurting.

00:22:12.400 --> 00:22:19.280
This hurts. Yeah. I considered that just a time penalty because as soon as I got that, I'm just putting the controller down until it wears off.

00:22:19.280 --> 00:22:21.260
Because I'm not even going to try and move.

00:22:21.840 --> 00:22:26.420
But the first time I did it, I was trying to...

00:22:26.420 --> 00:22:31.900
You know, when I realized I was reversed, even when it went back, it still took me a couple minutes to be like,

00:22:31.960 --> 00:22:32.780
is it back?

00:22:32.960 --> 00:22:35.640
Because it's still a little weird, even when it's normal.

00:22:36.160 --> 00:22:41.840
Your brain can't tell if it's adjusting to something or not.

00:22:42.320 --> 00:22:50.760
Yeah. And the whole time the game is obviously asking the question, just what are these snakes climbing to?

00:22:51.140 --> 00:22:54.900
You know, we're constantly going up this mountain.

00:22:55.240 --> 00:23:02.160
What is the end goal for these brothers or alleged brothers or people, snakes without names?

00:23:02.360 --> 00:23:03.600
Are they getting on a plane?

00:23:04.060 --> 00:23:06.420
They are going to the moon.

00:23:06.800 --> 00:23:08.660
Oh, I didn't gather that.

00:23:08.660 --> 00:23:10.840
That's how high this mountain was.

00:23:11.040 --> 00:23:12.820
It goes all the way to the moon.

00:23:13.020 --> 00:23:16.280
The last stage, everything shifts to this gray color.

00:23:16.460 --> 00:23:18.560
So I guess you're on the moon by that point.

00:23:19.880 --> 00:23:22.660
And eventually, when you get to...

00:23:22.660 --> 00:23:24.060
There's 10 stages.

00:23:24.360 --> 00:23:29.020
After the 10th stage, there's like a boss fight with that...

